  "title": "RB5X Personal Robot",
  "year": "1983",
  "subtitle": "A teachable autonomous mobile robot you program over a serial port — code made physical, with sensors and a memory for experience.",
  "why_scout_cares": "The museum's Topo (1983) is a desensorized floor robot that is pure output for code, and its HERO 1 is a keyboard-programmed educational robot. RB5X fills the missing middle of that triptych: a commercially sold autonomous mobile robot whose entire point is that it LEARNS — programmed remotely in TinyBASIC/Savvy over RS-232, then driven by eight bumper panels, a photodiode, and a sonic transducer that let it build behavior from experience. It is the era's cleanest commercial embodiment of code-meets-sensing-meets-physical-space, and the interaction model (talk to it through a cable, watch it develop) is genuinely distinct from everything else in the collection.",
  "search_terms": ["RB5X", "RB Robot Corporation", "personal robot 1983", "TinyBASIC robot", "autonomous mobile robot 1980s", "RB5X RS-232", "Heiserman robot"],
